---
// Virtual office guide 3. Legal points must be checked against gov.uk before publishing (Blueprint 04 · Check before the push).
import Guide from '../../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { address, formatPrice, virtualOffice, withCover } from '../../../data/facts';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../../data/guides';

const guide = guideBySlug('virtual-office-limited-company');
const [p20, p40] = virtualOffice.plans;
const dc = virtualOffice.directorsCover;

const title = "Virtual Office for Limited Companies | Maggie O'Neill Centre";
const description =
  "Yes, a limited company can use a virtual office as its registered office and business address. What Companies House accepts, and what to check first.";

const answer =
  "Yes. A limited company can use a virtual office as its registered office. It must be an appropriate address, where post reaches someone acting for the company. It can also be your business address and your directors' service address. If you work somewhere else, it won't usually count as your principal place of business for HMRC.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can a limited company use a virtual office address?',
    a: "Yes. A limited company can use a virtual office as its registered office. It can also be its business address and its directors' service address. The registered office has to be an appropriate address. That means post reaches someone acting for the company, and they can acknowledge it.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a virtual office address legal for Companies House?',
    a: 'Yes, if it’s a real address in a building with staff. It must be in the same part of the UK the company is registered in. Companies House doesn’t accept a PO box as a registered office.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I register a new company with a virtual office address?',
    a: 'Yes. You can give a virtual office as the registered office when you set up the company. You need the provider’s permission, and your AML checks with them must be done.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does a virtual office provider need to do an AML check?',
    a: 'By law, businesses that provide registered office services have to do anti-money laundering (AML) checks on their clients. It’s separate from the identity verification Companies House asks directors to complete.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use a virtual office for VAT registration?',
    a: 'HMRC asks for your principal place of business. That’s where the business is run day to day. If you run the company from home or somewhere else, that’s usually your principal place of business. This is true even if your registered office is a virtual office.',
  },
  {
    q: "How much is a virtual office for a limited company at the Maggie O'Neill Centre?",
    a: `A registered office and business address at the Maggie O'Neill Centre costs ${formatPrice(p20.price)} a month. Director's Cover adds a director's service address for ${formatPrice(dc.price)} a month. That takes it to ${formatPrice(withCover(p20))}.`,
  },
];
---
<Guide
  guide={guide}
  title={title}
  description={description}
  answer={answer}
  faqs={faqs}
  cta={{
    heading: 'A registered office for your limited company',
    text: `A real address at ${address.street}, Huyton, near Liverpool, from ${formatPrice(p20.price)} a month. We handle your post ourselves, and there are meeting rooms in the same building.`,
    href: '/services/virtual-office/',
    label: 'Virtual office Liverpool',
  }}
>
  <h2>What Companies House needs from a registered office</h2>
  <p>Your company's registered office can be a virtual office. It just has to meet the same rules as any other registered office:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It's an appropriate address.</strong> Post sent to the company would reach someone acting for it, and they could acknowledge it.</li>
    <li><strong>It isn't a PO box.</strong> It has to be a physical address.</li>
    <li><strong>It's in the right part of the UK.</strong> A company registered in England and Wales needs a registered office in England or Wales.</li>
    <li><strong>You have permission to use it.</strong> With a virtual office, that's your agreement with the provider.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A <a href="/services/virtual-office/">virtual office in Liverpool</a> in a building with staff meets all four. At the Maggie O'Neill Centre,
    our team handles virtual office post ourselves. We do it here at {address.oneLine}, and keep it securely stored.
  </p>

  <h2>What a virtual office can be used for</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Can it be a virtual office?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Registered office on Companies House</td><td>Yes, if it's an appropriate address</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Director's service address</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Business address on your website, invoices and letters</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Google Business Profile</td><td>Only if you meet customers there in your listed hours. Google's own rules decide this.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Principal place of business for HMRC</td><td>Usually not, if you run the business from somewhere else</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What to check before you sign up</h2>
  <h3>Your AML checks with the provider</h3>
  <p>
    Anyone who provides a registered office service has to check who their clients are. This is under the anti-money laundering rules. Expect to
    confirm who you are before the address goes live. At the Maggie O'Neill Centre, we send a secure form for this after you sign up.
  </p>
  <h3>Identity verification with Companies House</h3>
  <p>
    Companies House now also asks company directors and people with significant control to verify their identity. You do that with
    Companies House or an authorised agent. You don't do it with your virtual office provider.
  </p>
  <h3>What happens to your post</h3>
  <p>
    Official letters from Companies House, HMRC and the courts go to your registered office. So check how the provider handles post. Is it
    scanned? Can you collect it? Who tells you when it arrives? At the Maggie O'Neill Centre, every plan lets you collect your post from
    reception. The {p40.name} plan at {formatPrice(p40.price)} a month also has your post opened and scanned.
  </p>
  <h3>Whether you need premises</h3>
  <p>
    A few kinds of business need their own premises for a licence or registration. Some food, health and regulated financial
    businesses are like this. If that's you, check with the regulator before you rely on a virtual office.
  </p>

  <h2>Keeping your home address private</h2>
  <p>
    If you set the company up from home, your home may be on the public register. It may be there as the registered office and as your service address. A
    virtual office can replace it in both places. Every plan covers the registered office. {dc.name} adds the director's service address
    for {formatPrice(dc.price)} a month.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">
    More detail: <a href="/blog/home-address-companies-house/">how to keep your home address off Companies House</a> and
    {' '}<a href="/blog/registered-office-vs-business-address/">registered office vs business address</a>. Or see
    {' '}<a href="/services/virtual-office/">registered office address plans in Liverpool</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
